WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.440
Zack Snyder's Justice League has a lot going on in&nbsp;
its four-hour runtime, so it's not surprising if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:09.840
you miss a few things while watching it. These&nbsp;
lines go by quickly, but they're surprisingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.600
loaded with meaning. Here are some of the&nbsp;
key quotes from Zack Snyder's Justice League.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:21.680
"I've come to enlighten you to the great&nbsp;
darkness. I will bathe in your fear."

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.360
Most people would be intimidated by the sight of&nbsp;
a phalanx of battle-hardened Amazonian warriors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.800
but that's not the case for Steppenwolf.&nbsp;
When Darkseid's lieutenant breaches the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.880
stronghold on Themyscira, he doesn't&nbsp;
cower in fear. He taunts Hippolyta and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:38.960
her soldiers before unleashing a horde&nbsp;
of parademons on the vigilant defenders.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:41.120
A few minutes later, Steppenwolf repeats himself.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:44.480
"The great darkness begins."

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.440
If you're a longtime DC Comics fan, your&nbsp;
ears probably perked up immediately.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.600
Published in 1982, "The Great Darkness&nbsp;
Saga" was a five-part storyline that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:58.480
started in Legion of Superheroes #290. The&nbsp;
story pits a team called the Legion — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.680
group of 30th-century youths who have&nbsp;
modeled themselves after present-day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:05.600
superheroes like Superman — against a&nbsp;
mysterious villain called the Master.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.400
In one of the all-time greatest&nbsp;
reveals in superhero comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.680
the Legion eventually learns that the&nbsp;
Master is none other than Darkseid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:15.040
who has been waiting for 1,000&nbsp;
years for the opportunity to strike.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.240
To this day, The Great Darkness Saga&nbsp;
is still considered the very best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.240
Legion of Superheroes tale and one of&nbsp;
Darkseid's most memorable appearances.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:25.840
It's only fitting that it gets a nod&nbsp;
in Darkseid's feature film debut.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:33.040
"Wonder Woman. What do you think, man? You&nbsp;
think she'd ever go for a younger guy?"

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.880
Barry Allen's offhand, casually pervy quip&nbsp;
to Cyborg isn't all that notable on its own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.480
although Cyborg's reply is one of the&nbsp;
Snyder Cut's more memorable jokes.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:48.640
"She's 5,000 years old, Barry.&nbsp;
Every guy's a younger guy."

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:53.920
Surprisingly, this little exchange marks&nbsp;
a big moment in the history of the DCEU:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:57.680
It's the very first time in any of these&nbsp;
movies that Diana is actually called Wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.840
Woman on-screen. Seriously. She doesn't get&nbsp;
called by her superhero nickname in Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.880
v. Superman: Dawn of Justice, the Justice League&nbsp;
theatrical cut, or in either of her solo films.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:10.640
In the comics, of course, Diana has been going&nbsp;
by Wonder Woman since her mother bestowed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:16.400
title on her in her debut story, which appeared in&nbsp;
1941's All-Star Comics #8. In her next appearance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:21.920
1942’s Sensation Comics #1, shortly after&nbsp;
landing in man's world, Diana causes a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:26.320
scene as she outruns cars, beats up muggers,&nbsp;
and deflects bullets with her magic bracelets.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:31.280
This attracts the attention of an agent named&nbsp;
Al Kale, who signs Diana up for a stage show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.560
It's a hit, and the newspapers soon&nbsp;
dub her "Wonder Woman" publicly.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:39.760
Diana's partnership with Kale ends just a few&nbsp;
pages later, when Diana quits the show and Kale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:43.280
tries to run off with the profits. Still,&nbsp;
by that point, she's stuck with the name.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:45.760
"Suppose we start with your name."

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:46.400
"Wonder Woman!"

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:48.152
"Sure."

00:02:48.152 --> 00:02:51.760
"...and I'm happy to discuss with you, in&nbsp;
any way you like, why you sent a Boy Wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.280
to do man's job."

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:00.160
There's a very good reason why we&nbsp;
don't see Batman's trusty sidekick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.760
Robin, in Zack Snyder's Justice&nbsp;
League: He's been dead for years.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:07.840
Before the Snyder Cut, however, all we had were&nbsp;
oblique hints to that effect, like the Robin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:12.000
suit memorial in the Batcave and comments&nbsp;
from Snyder during promotional interviews.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.440
Now, thanks to Batman's heated exchange&nbsp;
with the Joker, Robin's death at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:19.200
hands of the Clown Prince of Crime&nbsp;
is explicitly Snyderverse canon.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.840
Of course, Joker killing Robin isn't exactly&nbsp;
a new idea. It's been done a number of times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.760
including in Frank Miller's The Dark Knight&nbsp;
Returns, which was a huge influence for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.240
Zack Snyder's Justice League. The most notable&nbsp;
example, however, occurs in the '80s storyline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:36.400
"A Death in the Family," which somehow ends&nbsp;
up being even more morose than Snyder's take.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:41.840
In Batman #427, the second installment of&nbsp;
the story, the Joker beats Jason Todd's Robin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:46.480
with a crowbar, then blows up a building with&nbsp;
Robin still inside. That's a huge cliffhanger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:50.880
but it gets even worse. Rather than letting&nbsp;
writer Jim Starlin decide Robin's fate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:55.120
DC Comics set up a 1-900 number hotline&nbsp;
which readers could call to decide whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.120 --> 00:04:00.400
Robin lived or died. Death won by a paltry&nbsp;
72 votes, sealing the Boy Wonder's fate.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.480
"You have the blood of the old gods in you."

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.760
Steppenwolf's half-taunting, half-admiring&nbsp;
observation during his first real showdown with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:14.960
Wonder Woman begs a question: If Zeus, Ares, and&nbsp;
the rest are the "old" gods, who are the new ones?

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:18.640
Well, comic readers already know the&nbsp;
answer. See, Darkseid, Steppenwolf,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:21.680
DeSaad, and the rest made their&nbsp;
debut in the Fourth World Saga,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:26.720
which introduced the New Gods to DC Continuity —&nbsp;
courtesy of master comic book artist Jack Kirby.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:29.920
The Fourth World is Jack Kirby at&nbsp;
his biggest and most imaginative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:34.240
and tells the story of a cosmic battle between&nbsp;
the forces of good and evil. On one side are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:38.400
residents of the planet New Genesis, which&nbsp;
is led by Highfather and his adopted son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:43.280
Orion. On the other, you have the forces of&nbsp;
Darkseid, who resides on the planet Apokolips.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:48.160
Kirby never really got to finish the story, which&nbsp;
was maybe more ambitious than entirely coherent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:51.840
Still, many of the New Gods characters&nbsp;
became fixtures of DC's canon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:55.840
so it's nice to see Steppenwolf pay subtle&nbsp;
tribute to the story that started it all.

00:04:56.388 --> 00:04:59.040
"Ship's all yours now, kid. You're the boss.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:06.160
Ryan Choi, director of&nbsp;
nanotechnology. That's your thing."

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.840
Ryan Choi spends most of Zack&nbsp;
Snyder's Justice League playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:12.160
second fiddle to S.T.A.R.&nbsp;
Labs director Silas Stone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:16.400
but the end of the movie sets him up for much&nbsp;
bigger — or should we say smaller? — things.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:20.880
In light of Silas' sacrifice, Ryan gets a&nbsp;
much-deserved promotion, but as this line hints,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:25.520
that's only the beginning for the scientist. See,&nbsp;
in the comics, Ryan Choi is better known as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:30.800
Atom, a superhero with the power to change size&nbsp;
at will (hence the whole "nanotechnology" thing).

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:35.440
Choi is actually the fourth superhero to take on&nbsp;
the mantle of the Atom, following the Golden Age's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:41.200
Al Pratt, Ray Palmer, and former Suicide Squad&nbsp;
recruit Adam Cray. He's also inexorably tied to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:45.840
the Atom's legacy. In the comics, Choi takes&nbsp;
over Ray Palmer's position at Ivy University&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:50.160
after the superhero goes missing, leading him&nbsp;
to Palmer's old "bio-belt," which harnesses the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:54.640
power of a miniature white dwarf star in order&nbsp;
to give its wearer size-changing abilities.

00:05:56.396 --> 00:06:00.080
The words that echo through Clark Kent's&nbsp;
head as he reclaims his superhero identity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:03.360
are a throwback to Man of Steel,&nbsp;
during which Russell Crowe's Jor-El&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:06.960
delivers a heartfelt speech to his son&nbsp;
while Superman flies for the first time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:11.360
It's more than that, though. It's also a&nbsp;
reference to All-Star Superman by Grant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:15.520
Morrison and Frank Quitely, one of the&nbsp;
very best Superman comics ever published.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:20.480
Published in 2006, All-Star Superman is a&nbsp;
streamlined, continuity-free take on the Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:24.960
of Steel that aims to tell timeless Superman&nbsp;
stories revolving around a simple concept.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:29.760
Thanks to Lex Luthor's machinations, Superman's&nbsp;
powers become overcharged by solar radiation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:34.000
He's never been more powerful. He's also&nbsp;
dying, with only a year left to live.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:37.440
Superman spends his remaining time&nbsp;
on Earth doing what he always does:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.000
working to make it a better place. Still,&nbsp;
even the Man of Steel has limits. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:45.840
solar radiation poisoning catches up&nbsp;
with Superman, and he begins to pass.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:50.800
That's when he encounters a memory of his father,&nbsp;
Jor-El, who delivers these same poignant words.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:55.520
They're not much, but they're enough.&nbsp;
Superman rallies and foils Luthor's schemes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.520 --> 00:07:00.240
then literally ventures into the center of the&nbsp;
sun, which he keeps burning for all of eternity.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:04.800
Jor-El's speech is already a perfect summation&nbsp;
of Superman's entire deal, but it becomes even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:08.640
more poignant given its placement in Zack&nbsp;
Snyder's Justice League. As in the comic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:12.640
it comes at the moment when Superman, treading&nbsp;
the line between life and death, re-dedicates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:16.800
himself to his original mission and truly&nbsp;
becomes the savior he was always meant to be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.960
As a tribute to one of the most popular Superman&nbsp;
stories ever told, it couldn't be better.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:24.240
"I hate Atlanteans as much as you Amazons do."

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:28.080
Zack Snyder's Justice League&nbsp;
establishes a long-standing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:32.240
rivalry between the Atlanteans and the&nbsp;
Amazons. While it never quite pays off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:36.480
it does give Wonder Woman and Aquaman a bit of&nbsp;
history together. As she explains in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:39.040
the two peoples once even&nbsp;
went to war with each other.

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:43.280
The idea of a long-standing rivalry between&nbsp;
Atlantis and Themyscira has come up a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:48.480
of times. The Atlantean-Amazonian war is one of&nbsp;
the centerpieces of DC's big Flashpoint event,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:52.960
in which Barry Allen creates an alternate timeline&nbsp;
by traveling to the past to save his mother.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:56.080
In that version of the timeline, a&nbsp;
war begins when Wonder Woman's mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:00.560
Hippolyta, is assassinated on Wonder Woman&nbsp;
and Aquaman's wedding day, sparking a conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:05.040
that sees the Amazons conquer Britain while&nbsp;
the Atlanteans sink most of Western Europe.

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:09.200
As in Zack Snyder's Justice League, however,&nbsp;
the two sides ultimately reach an understanding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:12.400
putting their differences aside&nbsp;
to stop an unhinged Thomas Wayne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:16.160
who serves as this reality's Batman.&nbsp;
It's not quite the same as in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:19.040
but at the very least, you can see&nbsp;
where Zack Snyder got the idea.

00:08:20.701 --> 00:08:25.404
"Who have you ever loved?"

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:30.720
While we'll never get to see Zack&nbsp;
Snyder's proposed Justice League sequels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:33.760
we know the basic outline of what&nbsp;
they were going to be about thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:36.880
largely to the dialogue in the&nbsp;
Snyder Cut's extended epilogue.

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:41.840
It all begins when Mera snaps at Batman after he&nbsp;
tries to console her about Aquaman's death — and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:44.480
to be fair, it's kind of inappropriate. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:48.480
losing loved ones is kind of Batman's&nbsp;
whole thing. As the Joker explains,

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:52.000
"He knows exactly what it's like to lose&nbsp;
someone he loves. You know, like a father.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.000
Like a mother... Like an adopted son."

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:58.160
There's one person who's very conspicuous&nbsp;
in her absence on the Joker's list, though:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:00.400
Lois Lane. Yep, that's right.

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:03.040
As Zack Snyder explained, in&nbsp;
his Justice League follow-ups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:06.240
we were going to learn that Bruce Wayne&nbsp;
had fallen for Superman's girlfriend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:09.840
making her death in the Knightmare timeline&nbsp;
all the more tragic to the Dark Knight.

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:13.120
"I'll never love anybody the&nbsp;
way that I love your son."

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:19.520
Making it even worse? Her death, which caused&nbsp;
Superman to break bad, is Batman's fault.

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:23.760
"How many alternate timelines do&nbsp;
you destroy the world because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:29.040
frankly, you don't have the&nbsp;
cojones to die yourself?"

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:33.920
Ironically, however, this line also hints&nbsp;
at how Batman was going to save things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:37.120
This is just an alternate timeline;&nbsp;
in Snyder's planned trilogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:41.520
the main one would be saved after Cyborg used&nbsp;
the Mother Boxes to send Flash back in time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:45.760
making sure that Batman died instead of Lois&nbsp;
and keeping Superman on the side of good.

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:49.520
Basically, Snyder's whole Justice League&nbsp;
trilogy is here in this one scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:50.960
It's just hidden in the dialogue.

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:52.960
"How'd you get the house back from the bank?"

00:09:54.560 --> 00:10:00.480
"I bought the bank. Congratulations, by the way."

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:05.520
Batman and Superman have spent most of their&nbsp;
time in the DCEU as enemies. Their first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:09.680
clash was so epic it lasted for an entire&nbsp;
movie. Their second, while much shorter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:13.840
came right after Superman had been resurrected,&nbsp;
and his mind was still on the fritz. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:18.000
by the end of Zack Snyder's Justice League,&nbsp;
the two are not just colleagues, but friends.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:23.040
Still, when Bruce slaps Clark on the back and&nbsp;
congratulates him, it's not immediately clear why.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:27.760
It's not for coming back to life — Bruce and the&nbsp;
rest of the Justice League did that. It's not for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:32.400
getting the family farm back, either; Bruce did&nbsp;
that, too. It could be because of his engagement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:37.120
to Lois Lane, but the more likely answer lies&nbsp;
in a few small details you might've missed.

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:41.200
Earlier in the movie, you can spot a pregnancy&nbsp;
test in Lois Lane's drawer. When this scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:45.680
begins, Lois carries a bassinet into the&nbsp;
Kents' farmhouse. Between those two clues, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:51.520
conclusion is obvious: Lois Lane is pregnant, and&nbsp;
a super-baby is on the way. Well, kind of super.

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:56.240
As Snyder has explained, his original plan was for&nbsp;
Lois and Clark's baby to be powerless, although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:01.200
he'd later take after his uncle Bruce's example&nbsp;
and strap on a cowl to become Gotham's new Batman.

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:05.040
The Snyder Cut's most memorable line isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:07.760
actually in the movie. It only&nbsp;
appears in one of the trailers.

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:14.640
"We live in a society where&nbsp;
honor is a distant memory."

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:19.920
If you're not perpetually online, you may&nbsp;
not have known what all the fuss was about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:22.480
For the rest of us, though,&nbsp;
it was a moment to remember.

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:26.800
While that phrase originated as a line on the&nbsp;
classic sitcom Seinfeld, it became associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:32.240
with the Joker thanks to memes surrounding 2019's&nbsp;
Joker, starring Joaquin Phoenix. As time has gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:37.200
on, it's become even more popular. Given that the&nbsp;
Snyder Cut is basically a four-hour piece of fan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:41.440
service anyway, people went rabid when it looked&nbsp;
like it was going to make its way into the movie.

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:45.120
While the line didn't make the final cut, Zack&nbsp;
Snyder still talked to the press about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:48.080
and he says the meme-ification&nbsp;
of the Snyder Cut was an ad-lib,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:51.760
so that buzzworthy trailer moment&nbsp;
was basically Jared Leto's fault.

